1
00:00:02,399 --> 00:00:17,946
Narrator: Opening night at Hotel Nginx. Ten thousand guests arrive at the same moment. Many of them are on slow phones, so each request trickles in, one word at a time. If the hotel gives every guest their own receptionist, it runs out of rooms, and memory, just waiting for slow talkers.

2
00:00:18,376 --> 00:00:28,018
Marta: I am Marta, the manager. Rudy, tonight we do not hire ten thousand receptionists. We hire a few, and teach them to juggle.

3
00:00:28,448 --> 00:00:31,609
Rudy: Why can't each guest have their own receptionist?

4
00:00:32,140 --> 00:00:50,734
Narrator: A traditional thread per connection server needs one operating system thread, with its own stack, typically megabytes, for every open connection. At ten thousand slow clients, the classic C ten K problem, that is gigabytes of stacks, and brutal context switching, most of it spent on connections that are simply idle, waiting on a slow network.

5
00:00:51,164 --> 00:01:07,213
Narrator: Nginx was built to answer exactly that. A small fixed number of worker processes, often one per core, each managing tens of thousands of connections through a non blocking event loop. An idle connection costs a small state struct, not a whole thread.

6
00:01:07,644 --> 00:01:33,269
Narrator: The words you need. A web server answers H T T P requests. A reverse proxy receives them and forwards them to your real app. An upstream, or backend, is a server nginx forwards to. A connection is an open link between client and server. An event loop handles whichever connection is ready next, never waiting on one. A timeout is how long to wait before giving up. And a load balancer spreads requests across backends.

7
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:43,006
Narrator: Think of nginx as a hotel receptionist who greets every guest, answers easy questions, the static files, directly, and sends the rest to the right department, the backends.

8
00:01:45,956 --> 00:01:58,718
Marta: Here is the rule of my hotel. I, the master process, never serve a guest. I read and check the rule book, I open the front doors, and I hire, watch, and replace the receptionists.

9
00:01:59,147 --> 00:02:13,662
Wes: I am Wes, a receptionist. A worker. One person, one thread, one event loop. I do not stand waiting at one guest. I watch a board of bells, and serve whoever rings.

10
00:02:14,092 --> 00:02:25,701
Narrator: That board is epoll, on Linux. The worker registers interest in many sockets at once, and the kernel says which ones are ready, so one thread serves many connections without ever blocking on a single slow one.

11
00:02:26,132 --> 00:02:42,590
Narrator: Two numbers set the ceiling. Worker processes, commonly auto, one per detected C P U core. And worker connections, how many connections one worker holds open. Their product is nginx's practical connection limit on one machine. One worker can even serve everything, if asked.

12
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:59,837
Marta: And when I change the rule book, nobody is thrown out. Nginx dash s reload. I hire new receptionists with the new rules, while the old ones finish their current guests, and then leave. Zero downtime, zero dropped connections.

13
00:03:00,268 --> 00:03:11,925
Narrator: The picture to remember. One master that never touches traffic. N workers that do all the work, each juggling many connections, switching to whichever is ready next. Never one worker per connection.

14
00:03:14,876 --> 00:03:26,798
Narrator: Now the hotel's floor plan, the config. Three drawers nested inside each other. The outer drawer, http, holds settings every site shares. Logging, gzip, the upstream blocks, and every server.

15
00:03:27,228 --> 00:03:44,550
Narrator: Inside it, a folder per website, a server block. It listens on a port, and matches a server name. Much like one tenant of the whole nginx. Inside each folder, an index card per U R L pattern, a location, says exactly what to do. Proxy it, serve a file, redirect it.

16
00:03:44,979 --> 00:03:59,469
Narrator: A request for https, api dot example dot com, slash orders slash forty two, takes exactly one path down this tree. First the server block whose server name matches api dot example dot com on that port. Then, inside it, one location.

17
00:03:59,900 --> 00:04:13,262
Narrator: Settings flow one way only, outer to inner. A location can override something from its http block. And an http level setting silently applies to every server and location below it, unless overridden.

18
00:04:16,211 --> 00:04:27,125
Bea: I am Bea, the bellhop. I read the signs. And here is my secret. I do not read them top to bottom, and stop at the first match.

19
00:04:27,555 --> 00:04:44,302
Narrator: Nginx applies a fixed precedence, wherever each block sits in the file. First, equals slash exact. An exact match, and the search stops at once. Second, caret tilde slash prefix. The longest matching prefix, and if it wins, regex checks are skipped entirely.

20
00:04:44,732 --> 00:04:57,613
Narrator: Third, tilde, or tilde star, a regex. Case sensitive or insensitive. The first matching regex, in file order. And last, a plain prefix. The longest plain match, used only if nothing above matched.

21
00:04:58,043 --> 00:05:12,053
Rudy: A trap, Bea. Slash images slash comes first in the file. A regex for dot jpg and dot png comes second. Which serves slash images slash logo dot png?

22
00:05:12,584 --> 00:05:26,978
Bea: The regex. Any matching regex outranks a plain prefix, however specific the prefix looks, once the plain prefix search is done. File order only breaks a tie between two regexes.

23
00:05:27,408 --> 00:05:42,257
Narrator: The common mistake. Reading locations like an if, else if chain. It is not. Exact beats everything. After that, a caret tilde prefix skips regex entirely. Otherwise, any matching regex beats any plain prefix.

24
00:05:45,208 --> 00:05:54,394
Narrator: Now the departments. A pool of backends is an upstream block. Orders, with three desks. Nginx forwards a request with proxy pass, to the pool.

25
00:05:54,824 --> 00:06:07,105
Oz: I am Oz, the orders department. Three desks, three addresses. Two are normal. The third is a backup, used only if the other two are down.

26
00:06:07,536 --> 00:06:22,314
Narrator: Nginx also adds headers. Proxy add x forwarded for appends the immediate client's address, rather than overwriting it. The same append only rule from the reverse proxy video. A backend reading it still trusts only as many hops as it controls.

27
00:06:22,744 --> 00:06:50,122
Narrator: And it can balance several ways, chosen per upstream. Round robin, the default, cycles in order. Weighted round robin, some servers get proportionally more. Least conn, to whichever has the fewest open connections. I P hash, the same client address always reaches the same server, which gives stickiness, but can leave load uneven. And a generic consistent hash, a custom key, like the U R I, maps to a server, minimising remapping when one is added or removed.

28
00:06:50,552 --> 00:07:06,434
Narrator: Health checks. Open source nginx only does passive checks. Max fails failures within fail timeout marks a server down for a while, and traffic stops going to it until fail timeout passes. So the first few real requests during an outage still reach the bad server.

29
00:07:06,863 --> 00:07:18,353
Narrator: Active checks, probing a health endpoint on a timer, are an Nginx Plus feature. Open source setups pair nginx with an external health checker, or let the orchestrator, like Kubernetes readiness probes, do it.

30
00:07:21,303 --> 00:07:32,841
Narrator: Eleven thirty. Orders slows down. Some guests see five oh four, gateway timeout. And the department's health desk, a trivial check, answers fine. Marta investigates.

31
00:07:33,271 --> 00:07:49,129
Narrator: Nginx has three different clocks. Proxy connect timeout, the time to open the connection to the upstream, five seconds. Proxy send timeout, the time between successive writes to it. And proxy read timeout, the time between successive reads from it, the default is sixty seconds.

32
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:59,129
Marta: And here is the catch. Read timeout is not one deadline for the whole request. It resets on every chunk nginx receives.

33
00:07:59,560 --> 00:08:11,649
Narrator: A backend that streams a few bytes every twenty seconds for five minutes never trips a thirty second read timeout, because each new chunk restarts the clock. A backend that goes fully silent for thirty one seconds does.

34
00:08:12,079 --> 00:08:16,802
Oz: My slow query takes forty five seconds before the first byte.

35
00:08:17,232 --> 00:08:32,658
Narrator: So at thirty seconds, nothing has arrived, and nginx gives up, and answers five oh four. When the backend finally replies at forty five, nginx has already closed that side, and discards the answer. A refused or reset connection, by contrast, gives five oh two.

36
00:08:33,087 --> 00:08:44,361
Marta: The fix is a per route timeout, tuned to that endpoint's real slow case, or fixing the slow query. Not a global increase that hides genuinely hung requests everywhere else.

37
00:08:44,792 --> 00:09:01,418
Narrator: And buffering. With proxy buffering on, nginx reads the upstream's response, up to the buffer limits, before forwarding it to a slow client, freeing the upstream connection quickly. Turn it off for real time streaming, like server sent events, where the client must see each chunk immediately.

38
00:09:04,368 --> 00:09:17,466
Narrator: The front door. A server block that listens on port four forty three with S S L, turns on H T T P two, and points at a certificate and a key. It prefers T L S one point three, with one point two as a fallback.

39
00:09:17,895 --> 00:09:29,817
Narrator: And it adds one header, strict transport security, max age thirty one million five hundred thirty six thousand seconds. That tells browsers, for a year, only ever speak H T T P S to this site.

40
00:09:32,768 --> 00:09:49,322
Narrator: Easy requests never reach a department. For slash static, nginx serves files from a root folder. G zip static serves a pre compressed dot g z sibling if one exists. And expires thirty days sets cache control, max age two million five hundred ninety two thousand.

41
00:09:49,751 --> 00:10:08,105
Narrator: For a hot endpoint, nginx can micro cache. Proxy cache valid, two hundred, five seconds. Proxy cache use stale, updating, error, timeout. A five second cache in front of a popular, rapidly repeated endpoint can turn thousands of identical requests per second into a handful per second upstream.

42
00:10:08,535 --> 00:10:14,865
Narrator: It is the same request collapsing idea as a C D N's origin shield, applied at a single reverse proxy.

43
00:10:15,296 --> 00:10:32,857
Narrator: But the safety window is narrow. A micro cache is safe for public, non personalised responses. Caching anything that varies per user, even for five seconds, risks the same private data leak as a C D N. The cache key must include every dimension the response varies on.

44
00:10:35,807 --> 00:10:53,706
Narrator: The bouncer at the door. Imagine a bucket with a small hole, draining water at a fixed rate, however fast you pour. Pour faster, and it fills. Faster still, it overflows. Those overflows are the rejected requests. That is a leaky bucket, and it is what limit req implements.

45
00:10:54,135 --> 00:11:15,177
Narrator: Requests drain at the rate, here ten per second per client I P. Burst allows a short spike to queue, instead of being rejected at once. No delay serves burst requests immediately, instead of spacing them out to the steady rate. Without no delay, queued burst requests are delayed to fit the rate. Past both, nginx returns five oh three by default.

46
00:11:15,607 --> 00:11:33,169
Narrator: An example. Rate ten per second, burst twenty, no delay. A client suddenly sends twenty five requests in the same instant. The first twenty are served immediately. The remaining five are rejected with five oh three. And none of the twenty are throttled to ten per second, because no delay is set.

47
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:49,985
Narrator: A different guard is limit conn. It caps simultaneous open connections per key, whatever the request rate. It defends against a client opening many slow, long lived connections. One request a minute over fifty connections trips limit conn, not limit req.

48
00:11:50,416 --> 00:12:03,322
Narrator: And a classic false alarm. Many users behind one corporate N A T share one public address, so they all count against one bucket. Key on an authenticated user I D or an A P I key where you can.

49
00:12:06,272 --> 00:12:12,337
Chip: I am Chip, the chat department. My guests keep the line open for hours.

50
00:12:12,768 --> 00:12:29,274
Narrator: A web socket starts as an H T T P request, then upgrades to a raw, long lived, two way pipe. Nginx must forward the Upgrade and Connection upgrade headers, and use H T T P one point one, to know to hold the connection open as a pipe, instead of a single request and response.

51
00:12:29,703 --> 00:12:41,241
Narrator: And raise the read timeout on that route, to thirty six hundred seconds. The default sixty seconds applies to web sockets too, and would drop every idle chat connection after exactly one minute.

52
00:12:44,192 --> 00:12:58,081
Narrator: Marta's complete rule book, condensed. An upstream named orders backend. Least conn. Two servers, each marked down after three failures within ten seconds. And keepalive thirty two, to reuse connections to the backend.

53
00:12:58,511 --> 00:13:10,889
Narrator: A rate limit zone, orders r l, ten megabytes of state, twenty requests per second. Then one server on four forty three, with H T T P two, the server name, the certificate, the protocols, and the strict transport header.

54
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:33,802
Narrator: Inside, the orders location. Limit req, burst forty, no delay. Proxy pass to the upstream. H T T P one point one, and an empty Connection header, so keepalive to the backend is not defeated. Host, real I P, forwarded for, and forwarded proto, passed along. Connect timeout five seconds, read timeout thirty. And a static location, thirty days.

55
00:13:34,231 --> 00:13:51,097
Narrator: One trap in there. Keepalive in the upstream block needs proxy H T T P version one point one, and a cleared Connection header. Miss either, and it silently falls back to one connection per request. H T T P one point oh has no persistent connections by default.

56
00:13:54,048 --> 00:14:17,586
Narrator: Other hotels. H A Proxy, an event loop with very low overhead, best in class load balancing and a stats page, often paired with nginx for static files. Envoy, built for dynamic configuration through x D S, a service mesh data plane, an A P I gateway. Caddy, automatic H T T P S out of the box. And Traefik, which discovers routes from Docker and Kubernetes labels.

57
00:14:18,015 --> 00:14:35,241
Narrator: A warning. The Kubernetes ingress nginx controller, a project built around nginx, was announced for retirement in November twenty twenty five, and stopped receiving releases and security updates in March twenty twenty six. New Kubernetes ingress should target the Gateway A P I.

58
00:14:35,672 --> 00:15:03,601
Narrator: The trade offs. Open source nginx load balancing is simpler than a dedicated L four balancer or a mesh: no active health checks, no per request retry budgets or circuit breaking out of the box. One event loop per worker is brilliant for I O bound proxying, but will not spread one C P U heavy request across cores. And its config is static, a reload is graceful, but still a file change. For deep observability and dynamic config, Envoy is usually better suited.

59
00:15:06,552 --> 00:15:27,234
Narrator: Five failures, and fixes. Five oh twos after a backend deploy. Passive checks need time to notice a bad instance, so also remove it from D N S or service discovery. Five oh fours on one slow endpoint, give that route its own read timeout. Web sockets dropping at exactly sixty seconds, raise that location's read timeout.

60
00:15:27,663 --> 00:15:43,306
Narrator: Keepalive to the upstream not reused, you are missing H T T P one point one, or the cleared Connection header. And limit req rejecting legitimate users behind a corporate N A T, key on a user or A P I key, not the shared address.

61
00:15:43,735 --> 00:16:04,177
Narrator: What to monitor. The four xx and five xx split by upstream, using upstream status in the logs, which is different from nginx's own response code. Upstream response time against total request time. Active, reading and writing connection counts from stub status. The rate limit rejection count. And worker connection saturation.

62
00:16:07,128 --> 00:16:27,450
Narrator: A few deeper points. First, why epoll beats select and poll at scale. Select and poll make the kernel rescan the entire set of watched file descriptors on every call, an O of n cost, however many are ready. Epoll keeps the set registered in the kernel and returns only the ready ones, so each check is proportional to ready events, not watched connections.

63
00:16:27,879 --> 00:16:40,570
Narrator: Second, why worker processes auto is usually right. One worker per core avoids both under use and needless context switching. On a machine also running other C P U heavy processes, you may deliberately use fewer.

64
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:57,145
Narrator: Third, why upstream keepalive needs proxy H T T P version one point one. H T T P one point oh has no persistent connection concept by default. Without bumping the version, nginx cannot keep that backend connection open, whatever the keepalive number says.

65
00:16:57,576 --> 00:17:11,178
Narrator: And last, micro caching's narrow safety window. Safe for public responses. For anything per user, the cache key must include every dimension the response varies on. Same rule as a C D N, a smaller blast radius.

66
00:17:14,127 --> 00:17:18,417
Rudy: Marta, test me. Six questions you will really get.

67
00:17:18,847 --> 00:17:22,849
Rudy: Why can one worker handle thousands of connections with one thread?

68
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:36,357
Marta: A non blocking event loop on epoll. The worker registers many sockets, and the kernel says which are ready. So one thread serves whichever connection is ready, never blocking on one slow client.

69
00:17:36,788 --> 00:17:46,814
Rudy: A trap. Slash images slash, then a regex for jpg and png, in that order. Who serves logo dot png?

70
00:17:47,344 --> 00:18:00,586
Marta: The regex. Precedence is exact, then caret tilde prefix, then the first matching regex in file order, then plain prefix. A plain prefix only wins if no regex matches.

71
00:18:01,016 --> 00:18:09,026
Rudy: A proxied endpoint returns intermittent five oh fours under load, but its health check is always fine. What do I check?

72
00:18:09,556 --> 00:18:20,614
Marta: The proxy read timeout, against that endpoint's real p ninety nine. A health check does trivial work. Give the route its own timeout, or fix the slow path.

73
00:18:21,044 --> 00:18:23,773
Rudy: Passive versus active health checks?

74
00:18:24,304 --> 00:18:38,554
Marta: Passive marks a server down only after real traffic fails, so the first requests during an outage still hit it. Active probes a health endpoint on a timer, independent of traffic. Active is Nginx Plus only.

75
00:18:38,983 --> 00:18:42,529
Rudy: Why does web socket proxying need extra config?

76
00:18:43,059 --> 00:18:54,933
Marta: It upgrades to a raw, long lived pipe. Nginx needs the Upgrade and Connection upgrade headers, and H T T P one point one, to hold it open, and a longer read timeout.

77
00:18:55,364 --> 00:18:59,701
Rudy: What does limit req, burst twenty, no delay, do?

78
00:19:00,231 --> 00:19:10,978
Marta: The rate is the steady leak. Burst lets twenty queue above it. No delay serves those at once. Past twenty above the rate, five oh three.

79
00:19:13,928 --> 00:19:21,961
Narrator: Midnight. The doors of Hotel Nginx open, and ten thousand guests flow in. Three receptionists, one manager, no queue, no crash.

80
00:19:22,392 --> 00:19:26,273
Marta: Ten thousand guests, and not one thread per guest.

81
00:19:26,703 --> 00:19:36,945
Narrator: So, in short. One master that never touches traffic, N workers, each a single threaded event loop on epoll. Worker processes times worker connections is the ceiling.

82
00:19:37,375 --> 00:19:47,713
Narrator: Two. Config nests http, server, location. Location precedence is exact, caret tilde, first regex, then plain prefix. Never first block in the file.

83
00:19:48,144 --> 00:19:57,761
Narrator: Three. Upstream plus proxy pass is the backend pool pattern. Least conn, I P hash, and consistent hash balance. Active health checks are Nginx Plus only.

84
00:19:58,192 --> 00:20:10,185
Narrator: Four. Three different timeouts. A silent upstream trips read timeout, five oh four. A refused or reset connection gives five oh two. Keepalive needs one point one and a cleared Connection header.

85
00:20:10,615 --> 00:20:22,609
Narrator: Five. Limit req limits rate. Limit conn limits simultaneous connections. Web sockets need Upgrade forwarding and a longer read timeout. And nginx dash s reload is zero downtime.

86
00:20:23,039 --> 00:20:31,938
Narrator: And the one line to keep. Nginx is a cheap front door. A few workers juggle many connections, and route each request to files or backends.

87
00:20:34,887 --> 00:20:52,521
Narrator: To go deeper, open the Nginx note in your vault. Before it, read forward and reverse proxies, and H T T P fundamentals. After it, T L S and H T T P S, and what happens when you type a U R L. Compare it with load balancing, rate limiting, and C D N and edge.

88
00:20:52,951 --> 00:20:55,298
Rudy: We opened on time, Marta.

89
00:20:55,728 --> 00:20:57,498
Marta: Next night.

